// pfodWeb NOTE: dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion audit, 2026-07-12.
// Board: uPesy ESP32-C3 Mini ("uPesy ESP32C3 Mini Low Power" in uPesy's own
// product listing; USB_PRODUCT string in this variant's pins_arduino.h reads
// "uPesy ESP32C3 Mini"), ESP32-C3-MINI-1 module on uPesy's smallest carrier
// (3cm x 2cm, no integrated connectors - pads only). This board's own
// pins_arduino.h declares TX=21, RX=20, SDA=3, SCL=10, SS=7, MOSI=6, MISO=5,
// SCK=4, A0=0, A1=1, A3=3, A4=4, A5=5 (generic ESP32-C3 template aliases -
// note A2 is NOT declared on this board, unlike the sibling Basic board),
// and VBAT_SENSE=0 (aliased to BAT_VOLT_PIN). No board.json override existed
// before this audit (used the chip-level esp32c3/board.json default).
// Flagged by the discovery sweep for having VBAT_SENSE=0 declared but never
// individually reviewed. Sibling board uPesy_esp32c3_basic (near-identical
// pins_arduino.h) was audited separately this same wave - NOT assumed
// identical; verified independently below, and a real difference was found
// (see the GPIO0 reasoning).
//
// Sources consulted:
//  - https://www.upesy.com/blogs/tutorials/upesy-esp32c3-mini-documentation-version-latest
//    Official uPesy documentation page for this exact board (Mini, not
//    Basic). States (French): "La carte intègre un pont diviseur de
//    tension, relié à la broche GPIO0, qui divise la tension V_DC par un
//    ratio." (The board has an onboard voltage-divider, wired to GPIO0,
//    which divides the DC voltage by a ratio.) Gives the measurement
//    formula verbatim: "V_BAT = 2 x (V_IO0,bits / 4095) x 2.5V", and
//    states "Le ratio est de 2." (The ratio is 2.) Also states the ADC's
//    usable range is considered limited to 0-2.5V on this chip ("La plage
//    de tension de l'ADC de l'ESP32C3 est limité entre 0 et 2.5V"), which
//    is why uPesy's own formula for this specific board uses a 2.5V
//    constant rather than 3.3V - this is a real, vendor-documented
//    difference from the Basic sibling board's own formula, which uses
//    3.3V (see uPesy_esp32c3_basic/board.json's pfodWeb NOTE). Also
//    confirms the reading is only valid when battery-powered (USB power
//    gives an incorrect result). Also confirms GPIO9 is wired to the BOOT
//    pushbutton ("La broche GPIO9 ... Elle est reliée au bouton BOOT."),
//    and the EN pin is wired to a physical RESET button (out of scope -
//    EN is not a usable GPIO). Also explicitly states there is no LED tied
//    to any specific pin on this or other "Low Power" uPesy boards
//    ("il n'y a pas de LED reliée à une broche particulière"), and no
//    power-on LED either.
//  - https://www.upesy.com/products/upesy-esp32c3-mini-low-power
//    Official product page. Confirms board identity/size (3cm x 2cm, no
//    integrated connectors) and independently confirms a battery-voltage
//    measurement circuit: "Elle dispose d'un circuit pour mesurer la
//    tension de la batterie." Does not itself give GPIO numbers.
//
// GPIO0 (VBAT_SENSE / BAT_VOLT_PIN / A0): confirmed onboard dedicated
// battery-voltage-sense pin via uPesy's own official Mini-specific
// documentation page and product page, with a vendor-given ratio of 2
// (equal-value resistive divider, R_series == R_shunt). Per this project's
// stated 3.3V-baseline convention for corrected ADC references (task
// formula: 3.3V * (R_series+R_shunt)/R_shunt), this gives an effective
// corrected reference of 3.3V * 2 = 6.6V - documented here even though
// uPesy's OWN formula for this specific board uses a 2.5V constant instead
// (see sources above; that 2.5V reflects uPesy's characterization of the
// ESP32-C3 ADC's practical linear range, a separate accuracy consideration
// from the ratio itself). Per this project's battery/voltage-monitor KEEP
// policy, this pin's capabilities are left unchanged from the chip-level
// default (analog_input retained, not excluded) - only a documentation
// "note" was added, matching the established pattern (see e.g.
// esp32c3/uPesy_esp32c3_basic's GPIO0, esp32c3/ttgo-t-oi-plus's GPIO2).
// adc.defaultRefVolts is NOT changed board-wide (still "3.3") because the
// divider is wired only to this one dedicated pin, not to the board's other
// analog-capable pins (A1/A3/A4/A5).
//
// GPIO9 (BOOT button, not named in this board's own pins_arduino.h):
// confirmed onboard physical BOOT pushbutton via uPesy's own official
// Mini-specific documentation page (GPIO9 wired to the BOOT button, used
// during firmware upload) - opportunistic section 7a check per this
// session's task. Also this chip's standard boot-mode strapping pin
// (chip-level pinNotes entry "Must be HIGH for normal boot" retained
// unchanged - expected/consistent with a button that pulls GPIO9 low when
// pressed). Tagged capabilities ["digital_input","button"] only, per the
// playbook's exclusion rule for button pins (no analog/output/pwm mixed
// in). No LED_BUILTIN or other LED alias exists in this file, and both
// vendor sources above explicitly confirm no onboard LED on this board -
// nothing to tag there.
//
// Alias-conflict check (per this session's playbook step): checked this
// board's own pins_arduino.h for any SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK/TX/RX alias
// landing on GPIO0 or GPIO9: SDA=3, SCL=10, SS=7, MOSI=6, MISO=5, SCK=4,
// TX=21, RX=20 - none is 0 or 9, so no bus-capability contamination risk
// and no pins_arduino.h line needed deletion for either pin.
//
// See boardsDetails/esp32/esp32c3/uPesy_esp32c3_mini/notes.txt for the
// full research trail.

{
  "boardName": "ESP32C3",
  "displayName": "ESP32-C3 Dev Module",
  "family": "esp32",
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },
  "dac": {},
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" },
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],
  "connections": ["serial", "ble", "tcp", "http"],
  "pinNotes": {
    "2": "Must be HIGH for normal boot",
    "8": "Must be HIGH or floating on reboot",
    "9": "Must be HIGH for normal boot"
  },
  "chipGpios": {
    "0":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"], "note": "VBAT_SENSE / BAT_VOLT_PIN - onboard battery-voltage sense (ratio-2 divider per uPesy's official Mini-specific docs; vendor's own formula for this board uses V_BAT=2*(bits/4095)*2.5V, equivalent to an effective 6.6V corrected reference using this project's 3.3V-baseline convention). Only valid when battery-powered, not on USB power. See pfodWeb NOTE above." },
    "1":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "2":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "3":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "4":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "5":  { "capabilities": ["analog_input_serial", "digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "6":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "7":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "8":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "9":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "Onboard BOOT pushbutton, confirmed via uPesy's official Mini-specific documentation page (GPIO9 wired to the BOOT button, used to enter firmware-upload mode). Also this chip's boot-mode strapping pin (pinNotes \"Must be HIGH for normal boot\" retained). See pfodWeb NOTE above." },
    "10": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "11": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "20": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] },
    "21": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output"] }
  }
}
